/**
 * The Swapngo mark, inlined as a data URI for Razorpay Checkout's `image`
 * option — the same square mark the admin console shows in its sidebar
 * (`apps/web/src/assets/logo-mark.svg`), exported to PNG as
 * `apps/mobile/assets/images/icon.png` (1024x1024).
 *
 * ── Why base64 and not a URL ─────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Razorpay's `image` accepts a URL or, in their words, "a base64 string if you
 * are not loading the image from a network". There is no public host for our
 * assets — the marketing site is not deployed and every Supabase bucket that
 * could serve it is private except `vehicle-model-images`, which is for
 * vehicle photos. A bundled asset path is useless here because Checkout is
 * NATIVE code: it cannot read a Metro asset reference.
 *
 * ── Why this is set in code at all ───────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The dashboard (Accounts & Settings > Checkout Styling) is the better home
 * for brand assets and still should be set — it covers Payment Links, Payment
 * Pages and receipts too. But it is an account-level setting that needs an
 * activated account, and until then Checkout renders a generated letter
 * placeholder: a bare "S". This makes the app self-sufficient. If the
 * dashboard logo is also uploaded the two agree, because they are the same
 * artwork.
 *
 * Regenerate with:
 *   node -e "console.log(require('fs').readFileSync('assets/images/icon.png','base64'))"
 */
export const BRAND_LOGO_DATA_URI =
    'data:image/png;base64,' +
    '';
